---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Little Blue Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Little Blue Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Eudyptula minor</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 25-30 cm (10-12 inches) tall, weighing around 1 kg (2.2 lbs). They are the smallest penguin species.</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Found along the coastlines of southern Australia and New Zealand.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Least Concern</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The Little Blue Penguin primarily eats small fish, squid, and other small marine creatures that they catch during short foraging trips close to shore.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Little Blue Penguins often nest in colonies, forming pairs that may return to the same nesting site year after year. They nest in burrows, rock crevices, or under vegetation. They are nocturnal breeders, coming ashore at dusk and returning to the sea before dawn, likely as a strategy to avoid predators. Their social interactions involve various calls and displays.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They are also known as the Fairy Penguin in Australia and the Kororā in Māori.</li>
                        <li>Their blue plumage is distinctive.</li>
                        <li>Little Blue Penguins are known for their evening "rafts" where groups gather offshore before coming ashore.</li>
                        <li>They have a relatively long breeding season, and some pairs may raise more than one brood in a year.</li>
                        <li>Conservation efforts focus on protecting their nesting sites from habitat destruction and introduced predators.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>